<title>USA: Weekend U.S. box office chart -- Nov 8 - 10.</title>
<headline>Weekend U.S. box office chart -- Nov 8 - 10.</headline>
<dateline>LOS ANGELES 1996-11-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following are the final data for the top 10 movies at the November 8-10 U.S. box office, according to Exhibitor Relations Co.</p>
<p>    Film		   Three-day gross    Cumulative Gross</p>
<p>1. Ransom		     $34,216,088	 $  ----</p>
<p>2. Romeo &amp; Juliet	   $ 9,026,448	 $ 23,538,658</p>
<p>3. Set It Off		 $ 8,812,105	 $ 11,807,451</p>
<p>4. Sleepers		   $ 3,716,065	 $ 43,581,596</p>
<p>5. High School High	 $ 3,082,069	 $ 16,759,976</p>
<p>6. The First Wives Club   $ 2,242,056	 $ 97,079,441</p>
<p>7. Larger Than Life	 $ 2,017,559	 $  6,636,784</p>
<p>8. The Ghost and</p>
<p>    the Darkness	    $ 1,727,222	 $ 34,575,614</p>
<p>9. Dear God		   $ 1,508,629	 $  5,555,525</p>
<p>10 Michael Collins	  $ 1,408,519	 $  8,182,610</p>
<p>NOTE: &quot;Ransom&quot; is released by Walt Disney Co. &quot;William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet&quot; is released by News Corp.'s 20th Century Fox. &quot;Set It Off&quot; is released by New Line Cinema, a unit of Time Warner Inc.'s  Turner Broadcasting System. &quot;Sleepers&quot; and &quot;Michael Collins&quot; are released by Time Warner's Warner Bros. &quot;High School High&quot; is released by Sony Corp's TriStar Pictures. &quot;The First Wives Club,&quot; &quot;The Ghost and the Darkness&quot; and &quot;Dear God&quot; are released by Paramount Pictures, a unit of Viacom Inc.. &quot;Larger Than Life&quot; is released by MGM, which is owned by financier Kirk Kerkorian and Seven Network Ltd.</p>
